ies, good. Let me just do a loop: right 150 (to col8), left 150 (back), up 150, down 150, right 20, left 20. This changes scroll/screen/position/animation bytes.\n\nThen snapshot and diff unchanged. That should remove a lot.\n\nThen: from there, journey to enemy screen (right ~350), take damage once, screenshot, snapshot. Diff decreased from the stable set.\n\nBut wait: the stable set from the safe loop—does it include health (6)? Yes as long as no damage. Does it include rupees/bombs/keys (0)? Yes. \n\nThen after damage, health 6→5. So diff decreased from stable set should yield health plus any bytes that happened to change (screen data changed during the journey to enemy screen — those would show as changed, but if they decreased they'd appear). Hmm, many screen-related bytes might decrease. But since we already filtered out bytes that changed during the safe loop, the remaining screen-related bytes might still change when going to a NEW screen (col 9) — those weren't exercised in the loop (loop covered col7→col8 only). Hmm.\n\nLet me instead make the safe loop cover all needed transitions INCLUDING going to the enemy screen but avoiding damage? Can't avoid damage there.\n\nAlternative: do the damage test on a screen reachable via the same transitions. E.g., the enemy screen is col9. My loop could go col7→col8, and I could also go col8→col9 and back without damage? Possibly get hit.\n\nHmm.\n\nAlternatively, use a totally different enemy damage source: maybe there's an enemy on a screen adjacent to start that's already covered.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Perhaps I'm overcomplicating. The damage diff hd1 had 146 survivors. Many were OAM (0x02xx) and PPU-related. I can simply avoid the OAM region by noting that health is likely in the $0600-$06FF or zero page. Let me look at hd1 hits in that range: 0x004A (2→1), 0x0060 (1→0), 0x009A (8→4), 0x0261... etc.\n\nHmm, 0x009A 8→4 (halved?). Not health.\n\nNo 6→5 anywhere in the shown 64. Let me get the full hd1 list by chaining a filter. Actually I can diff h_before→h_after with filter decreased AND restrict considered via from U1 (unchanged set from a different snapshot)? But U1 is from st1/st2 which are different bytes... from works by address survivors, so from U1 (1970 addresses) then decreased h_before→h_after → hits limited to those 1970, still 146. No help.\n\nBetter: Let me directly search for the health value by writing. But I need candidates.\n\nNew idea: use `diff_memory` with filter `equals` on the dead snapshot: health=0. But too many zeros.\n\nAlternative idea: Look at the earlier death: health 6→0. Also, at death, health byte = 0. Then after continuing (new game?), health resets to 6. Hmm.\n\nOK here's a cleaner idea to shrink the candidate list dramatically: \n- Snapshot at full health standing on enemy screen (health 6).\n- Take a hit → health 5, snapshot.\n- diff decreased = 146 survivors.\n- Reload state (full health). Take a hit → health 5, snapshot2.\n- diff decreased = another 146.\n- Intersect via from: diff from hd1 → should keep health (which decreases both times) and drop random OAM bytes.\n\nThen a third hit and intersect again. This uses independent random events, so intersections collapse. That's the skill's \"repeat the action and narrow\" approach. It should work even with pollution, since OAM bytes are random.\n\nLet me do that. I already have hd1 = 146. Let me reload, walk to enemy screen, take one hit, snapshot, diff from hd1.\n\nBut \"decreased\" needs the SAME a→b? No: diff_memory(a,b,filter,from) considers only addresses in `from` set, comparing a and b. So I need a new a,b pair at full health → one hit. a=h_before-like (full health snapshot on enemy screen), b=after one hit.\n\nI have h_before (full health, enemy screen) and h_after (5). hd1 = decreased. Now reload and reproduce: a2 = full health on enemy screen, b2 = after one hit. Then diff from hd1.\n\nLet me do it. Reload, walk right ~350 to enemy screen, screenshot to confirm, snapshot 