t value at 0x066F-0x0670: full = 0x22FF? no... Actually little-endian 16-bit at 0x066F = 0x066F + 0x0670*256 = 34 + 255*256 = 65314. Death: 32 + 0 = 32. Nonsense.\n\nWhat if it's 0x066F = \"hearts\" in some fixed representation and 0x0670 = \"fraction\"? At full: 34, 255. Hmm 34 = 0x22. Maybe it's not hearts but \"rupee count\"? No, rupees are 0.\n\nWait wait. Maybe the region read is actually offset because CPU $0660 maps to something else? No, it's RAM.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider: maybe 0x066F/0x0670 are not health. Maybe 0x066F=34 is the \"max hearts\" byte but stored as hearts in some other unit. Hmm.\n\nActually! I recall now: In Zelda 1, Link's health is stored as a single byte at $0670 in units of... and the max at $066F? No.\n\nLet me just experiment. Pause-redraw trigger established. Let me write 0x0670 = 0x00 while paused, then unpause+repause, screenshot. If hearts empty → it's health.\n\nBut at full health 0x0670=0xFF. If it's health with 0xFF = full (3 hearts) and 0x00 = dead, then writing 0x00 → death after unpause maybe. Writing 0x55 → 1 heart.\n\nLet me do it. Currently paused in inventory. Let me:\n- write_memory 0x0670 = 0xAA (should be ~2 hearts if linear from 0xFF=3hearts → 0xAA = 2 hearts).\nThen press start (unpause) 20f, screenshot, press start (pause) 20f, screenshot.\n\nActually simpler: after writing, press start twice with a screenshot after the second. But the first unpause resumes the game; the redraw happens on pausing again.\n\nLet me do: write 0x0670=0xAA. Then play_input start 20 (unpause), then start 20 (pause), then screenshot.\n\nHmm, but during unpause the game may recompute health? Maybe not.\n\nLet me also write 0x066F? Not yet.\n\nLet me save state first (in case of crash).\n\nLet's go."}
