mm, maybe the display hearts are drawn by reading 0x066F but the DISPLAY only changes the number of tiles if 0x066F crosses thresholds, and at 32 it's still \"3 hearts\"? But at the damaged state earlier (after 1 hit, 0x066F=33) the display showed 2.5 hearts. Hmm.\n\nLet me just do the definitive test: write a very low value like 0x01 to 0x066F and see the hearts. If they disappear, 0x066F drives it; if not, something else.\n\nBut careful: writing 1 might not be a valid health and could glitch. Let me write 0x1C (28) = 0 hearts by val-31, or 0x10.\n\nActually, better: write 0x066F=0x1E (30) which by an unknown mapping should show ~0-2 hearts. Hmm.\n\nLet me think about what value gives a clear \"fewer hearts\". Given full=34=3 hearts, each heart likely = 1 unit? Then 31 = 0 hearts. Let me write 0x066F=0x1F (31) and inspect the heart tiles. Earlier screenshot 07e823 (0x066F=31) I thought showed 2 hearts. Let me instead directly inspect the nametable/pixels now.\n\nLet me set 0x066F=0x1F, 0x0670=0, redraw, then read nametable cols 22-24 row 6 and sample pixels. If all three still full hearts (tile 358), then 0x066F doesn't drive the display.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually — maybe the pause screen does NOT redraw the HUD hearts from the current health at all; it might draw them based on a cached OAM/nametable from before, and the pause screen just re-displays the previously-drawn HUD. Since the HUD is drawn on the playfield nametable and the pause screen scrolls to show it, maybe the hearts shown are whatever was drawn earlier (stale). And when I unpause+repause, the game might not redraw the HUD hearts.\n\nThat would explain: the HUD hearts only change when the game's damage routine redraws them. My writes to 0x066F don't trigger the redraw, so the pause screen shows the last-drawn hearts (full, from the save state).\n\nYes! That makes sense. The pause screen just shows the existing HUD (scrolled), not a fresh draw. So the pause screen is NOT a reliable redraw trigger for the counters/hearts.\n\nHmm, but wait, earlier I wrote 0x0670=0xAA and the pause screen showed 3 hearts; and after 0x066F writes it also showed 3 hearts. So no redraw. And the \"2 hearts\" in image 07e823 might have been my misreading, or maybe the game DID redraw because of some event.\n\nHmm, but earlier, at 0x066F=32, the pause HUD showed \"1 filled + 2 empty\" per my reading of 9357ff. If the HUD were stale-full, it would show 3 full. Hmm.\n\nI clearly can't trust my readings. Let me use the tile oracle: read nametable row6 cols 22-24 and read_tile for each. Currently at 0x066F=32 the tiles are all 102 → chr 358 = FULL heart. So display = 3 full hearts.\n\nLet me now change to another value and re-read the nametable (after unpause/repause) to see if it changes. If it stays 3 full, the pause screen is stale.\n\nActually, an even better redraw trigger: the game redraws the HUD when health changes (damage). So to verify 0x066F is health, write it and then take damage; the damage routine reads 0x066F to compute the new health and redraws. If I write 0x066F=10 and take a hit, the new display would be based on 10.\n\nBut that's what the trace will show.\n\nLet me step back and be systematic with the tile oracle (definitive):\n- The heart row is nametable row 6, cols 22-24, tiles map to chrRam 358=full heart. There must be a half-heart and empty-heart tile in chrRam too. Let me find them by scanning chrRam tiles? Hmm.\n\nActually, the game likely uses different tiles for full/half/empty. Let me find them: read the nametable after Link has taken damage (so display shows partial). Earlier snapshots of the game screen showed partial hearts. But now the HUD might be stale.\n\nOK let me change strategy entirely for hearts, using trace_writes with a narrow watch on the damage routine.\n\nActually, better: let me use the trace_writes data I already have! In that trace (400 frames, watching 0x0600-0x06FF), I didn't see writes to 0x066F/0x0670 because no damage occurred in that window? Actually the trace showed only enemy slot updates (0x0600-0x061D). So no hit happened during the trace.\n\nLet me redo the trace but ensure damage happens: go to the enemy screen, then trace writes on 0x0660-0x067F (32 bytes) until a hit occurs (the hit will write the new health). 60 frames might miss; use 600 frames. The health write should appear.\n\nActually wait, the trace callback watches writes; the damage routine writes health. Let me watch 0x0660 length 32. But if health is 0x066F, good.\n\nHmm, but the earlier watch showed 0x066F changing on hits, so the damage routine writes it. Let me trace and confirm.\n\nLet me also watch a wider range 0x0600-0x06FF again but with 600 frames to ensure a hit. The output will show the health write (a PC writing 0x066F with the decremented value). That PC is the damage routine, which also writes to the MAX hearts? Possibly not.\n\nBut more importantly, I want to find max hearts. The HUD draw routine reads max. Trace READS while the HUD is drawn would reveal it. The HUD is drawn: at game start, on damage, on item pickup, on pause? Since the pause screen might not redraw, use damage as the draw trigger.\n\nTrace reads mode on 0x0000-0x07FF during a damage event in chunks: the draw routine reads health (0x066F) and max hearts... but max might be stored elsewhere. The draw routine's reads would reveal addresses. But tracing all reads of 256 bytes over 600 frames = huge output.\n\nAlternatively, trace reads specifically of 0x0660-0x067F during a HUD redraw; we'd see 0x066F read. Max might be in that range if it exists. We saw all zeros there except 0x066F,0x0670,0x067C. Hmm.\n\nMaybe max hearts for the FIRST quest is not stored in RAM at all but derived from the save's \"heart containers\" count in SRAM, or it's a constant in the code plus a count. Hmm.\n\nActually, in Zelda 1, I believe there IS a byte for max hearts. Let me reconsider the region: maybe it's in 0x0400-0x04FF (the \"player\" variables?). We read only 0x0660-0x068F. Let me look at 0x0400-0x04FF or 0x0000-0x00FF.\n\nGiven 0x066F=34=0x22 and 0x0670=0xFF at full health, maybe the max hearts is 0x22's relationship... Hmm.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe BOTH 0x066F and 0x0670 together are just \"health\" and there is a separate max-hearts byte we haven't","truncated":true}
