{"type":"thinking","text":"Now I can see the game HUD:\nRow 3: cols 12 = 33, 13 = 0/36? (the rupee icon area?), 15-20 = 105,11,107,105,10,107 (the \"X0\" digits?), 23-28 = 98,21,18,15,14,98 = \"-LIFE-\" text.\nRow 5: cols 12=33, 13=0, 15-20=108,36,108,108,36,108 (X0 digits), 11=249.\n\nHmm, where are the hearts?! I don't see tile 102 (full heart). The hearts must be drawn as sprites or via a different mechanism.\n\nWait, maybe I'm reading the wrong nametable. get_tile_at_pixel said nametable 0. But there are 4 nametables; the HUD might be at nametable 2 (0x2800) with vertical mirroring? Hmm, get_tile_at_pixel handles it.\n\nLook at row 3-5 cols 15-20: tiles 105,11,107 / 108,36,108 — these are the \"X0\" graphics for rupee/key/bomb counts. Actually the rupee \"X0\" is at cols 15-17? and key \"X0\" at cols 18-20? and bomb at ... hmm.\n\nWhere are the hearts? Possibly at cols 24-31 rows 4-5 but they're 36 (blank). Hmm.\n\nWait — maybe the hearts are drawn as SPRITES after all. On the game screen, list_sprites might show 3 heart sprites. Earlier list_sprites (pause screen) showed only 3 sprites. Let me run list_sprites now on the game screen.\n\nActually, the hearts in Zelda 1 are background tiles, I'm pretty sure. But the nametable shows nothing.\n\nHmm, wait: the hearts in the image are at the top-right and appear BELOW \"-LIFE-\". Row 4 cols 24-31 are blank. So where?\n\nLet me just get_tile_at_pixel at the actual heart location. I need the correct pixel. Let me find the heart by scanning the game screen for the red color (0x16) or peach (0x36). Actually the full heart tile 358 (chrRam) is all 3s = palette index 3 = peach 0x36! So the FULL heart is drawn in peach?? That seems wrong; hearts are red.\n\nHmm, tile 358 chrRam was all 3s. But palette 1 color 3 = 0x36 peach. So the heart would be peach. The HUD hearts in the image look red/pink. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe the tile 358 I read is not the heart but part of the \"-LIFE-\" letters? No, it was a clear heart shape.\n\nHmm, palette 1 = [15, 22, 39, 54] = backdrop black, 0x16 red, 0x27 orange, 0x36 peach. A heart with all pixels = 3 would be peach — but hearts look red. Maybe the game rewrites the heart tile in chrRam each frame using color index 1 (red) for filled and 3 for empty? Since chrRam tile 358 currently is all 3s, that would be the EMPTY heart! And a full heart would be all 1s (or a different tile).\n\nSo tile 358 = empty heart outline (all 3s? no, an empty heart should be an outline, not solid). Hmm, the read showed a solid heart shape of 3s (filled). If 3=peach, a solid peach heart = \"empty heart\" (peach fill). A full heart might be red fill.\n\nSo the HUD hearts in the pause screen were all SOLID peach because they were empty?! Hmm, but they looked red-ish in the thumbnail.\n\nOK regardless: the game rewrites chrRam heart tiles to indicate fill. So the nametable tile id stays the same; the FILL is encoded in the CHR RAM graphics. That means I can't use the nametable to read health.\n\nSo to count hearts from the oracle, I need to read the chrRam tile (the heart row's tile) and see if it's the \"full\" pattern (red, index 1) or \"empty\" (peach, index 3) or \"half\". But that requires knowing which chrRam tile the HUD uses.\n\nThis is getting deep. Let me step WAY back and reconsider the whole approach, because I've spent a lot of effort.\n\nKey realization: My earlier differential search DID find health-related bytes (0x066F, 0x0670). The trace/watch confirmed 0x066F steps down on damage. So 0x066F IS a health variable. The exact display mapping is a detail.\n\nFor the deliverable, I should:\n1. Find rupee/bomb/key addresses.\n2. Find hearts (current health, and max containers).\n3. Prove each by writing and showing the HUD.\n\nFor proof, the pause screen might not be reliable, but the natural in-game HUD updates are. Actually, we saw that writing 0x066F=31 changed the game-screen HUD (image 7a2e21 showed 2 hearts vs 3 before). Wait, did it? The game screen after writing 0x066F=31 showed 2 hearts; but the game might have redrawn because of damage? No damage occurred. Hmm, maybe the game redraws the heart row every frame from 0x066F! Then writing 0x066F directly changes the display live. That would be a great proof: write 0x066F=32 and watch hearts.\n\nActually, the image 7a2e21 showed 2 hearts and the START image (full) showed 3 hearts. And the game was running when I wrote 0x066F=31. So the HUD did reflect the write. Good — so the hearts DO update live from 0x066F (or the game redraws them).\n\nBut then why did writing 0x066F=32 (earlier) not clearly show 1 heart? Maybe it did and I misread; and the pause screen showed 3 tiles because the pause HUD is stale or because the pause screen reuses the last-drawn HUD.\n\nOK so for heart health: 0x066F. Let me now determine the mapping by writing values and reading the actual rendered pixels. Since the hearts are drawn from chrRam tile that the game updates, let me instead find the heart SPRITES... \n\nHmm, actually let me reconsider: maybe the hearts ARE sprites. On the game screen, list_sprites would show them. Let me run list_sprites now.\n\nLet me do that. Also count sprites.\n\nActually, let me use a completely different and reliable oracle: get_pixel_color at the heart location. I need the location. From the game screenshot 7a2e21, hearts are at top right. Let me find red pixels by scanning. The full heart is red (0x16), the empty is peach (0x36). If writing 0x066F changes red↔peach, I can detect.\n\nLet me find the heart x,y. The \"-LIFE-\" text is at nametable row 3, cols 23-28 (x=184-231). The hearts are likely just below at row 4/5, same cols. But those are blank (tile 36). So hearts must be drawn as sprites or the game draws them in chrRam with scrolling... \n\nHmm, wait: maybe the HUD hearts are at nametable rows 3-5 cols 10-13? No, that's the minimap area (245s).\n\nUgh.\n\nLet me just run list_sprites."}
