th. Maybe the death routine sets 0x066F to 0x20 (1?) hmm.\n\nActually maybe at death the game sets health to 1 heart? No, game over.\n\nAlternatively 0x066F = 0x20 + full_hearts, and the half-heart is in 0x0670. So:\n- 0x066F=0x22 → 2 full? but display 3. Hmm.\n\nUgh.\n\nAlternatively 0x066F = 0x20 + half_hearts - 1? full = 6 half → 0x25. no.\n\nLet me just directly measure with a clean oracle: the number of FULL heart tiles (242) in row 6 cols 22-27 after a stable redraw. To force a stable redraw, I'll pause, write, then step 200 frames (the HUD might redraw periodically), then read. Actually, the HUD may only redraw on events, so stepping won't redraw.\n\nBetter: force a redraw by causing an event that redraws the HUD, like scrolling to a new screen or taking damage. Scrolling: the HUD redraws on screen transition? Probably not.\n\nHmm. Actually, the HUD IS likely redrawn every frame in many NES games? No.\n\nLet me test whether the HUD redraws on pause: pause the emulator, then press start? can't pause+input. \n\nOK, let me just use a completely different and definitive method for ALL FOUR variables: DISASSEMBLY-LITE via trace_writes on the HUD routine. Specifically:\n\nThe HUD update routine is called when a counter changes. It reads the counter and writes the tiles. If I trace WRITES to the nametable/PPU while a rupee is collected, I'd find the code. Too indirect.\n\nTime check: I've used a lot of steps. Let me refocus and be pragmatic.\n\nThe user's actual ask: find RAM addresses of rupee count, bomb count, key count, and Link's hearts. I need to deliver addresses + encoding + proof.\n\nGiven the difficulty of zero-valued counters, the write-bisect approach with the HUD as oracle is the intended path (the skill covers it). And we've established:\n- The HUD hearts respond to writes at 0x066F/0x0670 (verified).\n- The HUD counters (rupees/keys/bombs) are drawn from RAM somewhere.\n\nActually, here's a MUCH better idea for redraw: press Start to open the inventory. Does the inventory screen redraw the HUD from RAM? Earlier evidence suggested maybe. But we saw the pause screen showed 3 full hearts even when 0x066F=32 — suggesting stale. Hmm, but maybe the pause HUD is drawn from a DIFFERENT variable that we didn't change. \n\nWait, actually — maybe the pause screen's HUD row is drawn from RAM and my earlier \"3 hearts\" reading was wrong because the pause screen displays a DIFFERENT thing. Hmm.\n\nLet me test the pause-screen redraw definitively: set 0x066F=32,0x0670=0 (which we now believe → few hearts), then open the inventory and inspect the heart tiles (which in the pause screen are at nametable row 6 cols 22-24 as we found, tiles 102 = empty or 242 = full). Earlier at 0x066F=32 the pause screen showed 102,102,102 (all empty). And at full it showed 242,242,242. So the pause screen DOES reflect 0x066F! Wait, earlier at 0x066F=32 the pause screen nametable read gave \"102 102 102\" — yes! That was in the read at x=20 y=6: cols 22,23,24 = 102. And that was at 0x066F=32! And now on the game screen at 0x066F=32 we also got 102,102,102. Consistent! Great.\n\nSo the pause screen redraws from RAM. And earlier the \"3 hearts\" visual was at 0x066F=32? No wait: at 0x066F=32 I took screenshot 2fc4d3 and thought \"3 hearts\". But the nametable said 102,102,102 = all empty. So my visual reading was wrong (empty hearts are peach and I mistook them for full). OK! Good lesson: use the nametable/color oracle, not my eyes.\n\nNow, the encoding: let me establish it with the pause screen oracle. But that requires closing/writing/opening. Alternatively, the game screen HUD also reflects writes after stepping (the game redraws on some cadence). We got 102,102,102 at 32; 242,242,102 at 33 (2 full); 242,242,242 at 34 (3 full). And at 31 we got 242,242 (2 full) — inconsistent, probably stale.\n\nHmm, but 32→0 full, 33→2 full, 34→3 full. That's weird (32→0, 33→2, 34→3). Maybe the game redraws only the CHANGED hearts and my writes confuse it. Let me get a clean read for each value by opening the pause screen (full redraw).\n\nLet me do the pause-screen oracle test for 0x066F ∈ {30,31,32,33,34,35}. But opening the pause screen is a redraw at that instant; the value must be written before opening.\n\n","truncated":true}
