{"type":"thinking","text":"My writes persisted: 0x066F=34, 0x0670=0. Yet the HUD (after stepping 60) shows 242,242,102 = 2 full + 1 empty.\n\nHmm, so with 0x066F=34 and 0x0670=0, display = 2 full. And with 0x066F=32, 0x0670=0, display = 0 full. With 0x066F=34, 0x0670=0xFF, display = 3 full. With 0x066F=33, 0x0670=0xFF, display = 2 full.\n\nLet me tabulate (h = 0x066F, f = 0x0670):\n(34, 255) → 3\n(33, 255) → 2\n(34, 0) → 2\n(32, 0) → 0\n(31, 0) → 2 (stale?)\n\nHmm. (34,0)→2 and (32,0)→0. So decreasing h by 2 changed 2→0. And (34,255)→3 vs (34,0)→2.\n\nMaybe the display uses 0x0670 for the fraction and 0x066F for the integer, but the game's update routine converts: number of hearts = (h - 0x1C)/? \n\nLet's define full_hearts_display. Data:\nh=34,f=255 → 3\nh=33,f=255 → 2\nh=34,f=0 → 2\nh=32,f=0 → 0\n\nTry display = (h - 0x1F) when f>=0x80, and (h - 0x20) when f<0x80? \n- (34,255): 34-31=3 ✓\n- (33,255): 2 ✓\n- (34,0): 34-32=2 ✓\n- (32,0): 32-32=0 ✓\nThat fits all four! And (31,0): 31-32=-1 → 0, but we saw 2 (stale).\n\nSo the encoding: \n- 0x066F = hearts + 31 (if half present) or hearts + 32 (if empty)? That's equivalent to: health in half-hearts = 2*(h - 31) - (f<0x80 ? 1 : 0)? Let's compute:\n  - (34,255): 2*(3)-0=6 half = 3 hearts ✓\n  - (33,255): 2*(2)-0=4 half = 2 hearts. But natural after 1 hit showed 2.5. Hmm.\n  \nHmm, this model says (33,255) = 2 hearts (4 half), while natural after 1 hit was (33,127) = 2*(2)-1 = 3 half = 1.5 hearts. Hmm.\n\nWait, f=0x7F < 0x80, so (33,127): 2*(33-31)-1 = 4-1 = 3 half = 1.5 hearts. But natural display after 1 hit I thought was 2.5. Ugh.\n\nHmm, maybe the formula is: health_half = (h - 0x1F) * 2 - 2 + (f>=0x80 ? 1 : 0)? Let's just fit:\n- (34,255) → 6 half (3 hearts)\n- (33,255) → ? \n- (34,0) → ?\n- (32,0) → 0 hearts\n\nData with f=255: h=34→3, h=33→2. So each unit of h = 1 heart when f=255.\nData with f=0: h=34→2, h=32→0. Each 2 units of h = 1 heart when f=0?? (34→2, 32→0: slope 1 per 2). Hmm inconsistent.\n\nMaybe I misread (34,0)→242,242,102. Could the third tile 102 have been a leftover from the previous state (h=32,f=0 gave 102,102,102) and the redraw only updated 2 cells? If the HUD redraw updates only changed hearts, then going from (32,0)=102,102,102 to (34,0) it would set... hmm.\n\nI think the HUD redraw is incremental and my artificial writes produce states the game never intends, making the incremental logic inconsistent. The natural damage path is the only reliable data.\n\nLet me get the natural path data cleanly: reload, go to enemy screen, and read the heart row at full health and after each hit. I'll use the watch to know how many hits, and read the nametable. That's several calls but reliable.\n\nActually, better: instead of reading the nametable, read the chrRam heart tiles? No, the tile IDs tell full/empty. Let me just read the nametable row6 and the two values 0x066F/0x0670 after each hit.\n\nPlan:\n1. reload, walk right ~300 to enemy screen (full health: expect 242,242,242 and (34,255)).\n2. read nametable row6 + read 0x066F/0x0670.\n3. take a hit (walk into Octorok), read again.\n4. take another hit, read again.\n\nLet me do it. This will give the natural encoding.\n\nHmm, but I already did a natural hit run earlier. Let me just redo efficiently.\n\nActually — the KEY realization: for the deliverable, I mainly need the address(es) and a provable encoding. Given the mess, maybe the cleanest claim:\n- Link's health is at 0x066F: value = 0x1F + number of full hearts plus... hmm.\n\nLet me just do the natural experiment. Reload and go.\n\nActually, let me reconsider using the pause screen redraw to get CONSISTENT full redraws. Does opening the inventory do a full HUD redraw? Let me test: set (34,0), open inventory, read heart row. If it shows 242,242,242 (3 full), then the pause redraw is authoritative and my earlier incremental confusion is resolved. Test: currently paused with (34,0) and game screen shows 242,242,102. Let me resume, press start (open inventory), read.\n\nBut the game is paused now. play_input resumes. Let me: play_input start 20 (open inventory), read nametable.\n\nLet me do that."}
